  "ai_language_tip": "Dhivehi (dv) is spoken at home but is not on Bageo's list; English is taught in schools and used in business, so the AI should simply speak English.",
  "english_ok": "yes — Resorts, guesthouses, clinics and most Malé shops work in English; some island grocers and fishermen are more at ease in Dhivehi.",
  "politeness": "Friendly but reserved; a polite 'assalaamu alaikum' or plain hello works, keep dress-code and alcohol topics neutral, and thank with shukuriyya.",
  "name_tip": "Many people go by a given name plus a house name, and on small islands people are identified by their house; spell names carefully, as English spellings vary.",
  "phone_tip": "Seven-digit numbers with no trunk zero; mobiles start 7 (Dhiraagu) or 9 (Ooredoo), Malé landlines start 3, atoll landlines start 6; read as 3+4 (912 3456).",
  "time_tip": "24-hour clock is normal in writing; resorts often run on 'island time' an hour ahead of Malé time, so confirm which clock a pickup uses.",
  "number_tip": "Local shops price in rufiyaa (MVR); resorts, dive centres and tours price in US dollars; ask which currency each time.",
  "avoid_calling": "Avoid prayer times, when Malé shops close briefly, and Friday late morning to early afternoon; during Ramadan, daytime hours shrink.",
  "closed_days": "Weekend is Friday and Saturday; many shops reopen Friday afternoon; Eid holidays close offices for days.",
  "booking_channels": "Viber and WhatsApp carry most business chat; resorts book through their websites, email and big travel platforms; speedboat and seaplane transfers are set by the resort; ferries run on public timetables.",
  "voice_menu": "Telcos (Dhiraagu, Ooredoo) and banks have Dhivehi/English IVR; small shops and guesthouses answer directly or reply on Viber.",
  "callback_habits": "Guesthouses and dive shops reply by Viber or WhatsApp; clinics call or text the patient with the slot.",
